{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "taste_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.92,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A fine-grained taste categorization gives a strong actionable axis for improving product scores. Values track source text well (good/excellent/medicinal reviews mapped sensibly). The 'metallic_chemical_aftertaste' for the 'very medicinal' flavor is a reasonable read. Slight risk of over-specific negative buckets, but grounded on samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "flavor_intensity_alignment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.82,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Distinguishing too_weak/too_strong/ideal is a genuine actionable lever for reformulation. Silent rows correctly marked Unknown. 'ideal' assigned to rows with no explicit intensity comment (e.g. dog food) is a mild over-assertion, but generally defensible; medicinal->too_strong is warranted."
    },
    {
      "name": "price_value_fit",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.88,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Value/price perception is a useful distinct grouping axis for score drivers. Silent rows correctly Unknown. However 'good_value' assigned to the dog food and confection reviews that make no price statement is a mild unsupported inference; taffy 'great price' is well grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "product_freshness",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.83,
      "source_groundedness": 0.62,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Freshness/degradation is actionable for supply-chain/quality actions. But grounding is weak: nearly every sample defaulted to 'good' or 'excellent' even when the text says nothing about freshness (dog food, confection, peanuts, medicinal soda). This asserts a positive freshness state the text does not support rather than using a null/unknown, indicating fabricated confidence."
    },
    {
      "name": "expectation_mismatch",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.88,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Highly actionable for identifying score-hurting gaps between description and product. Values are well grounded: 'Not as Advertised' peanuts -> description_inaccurate, medicinal soda -> flavor_different_than_expected, positive reviews -> meets_expectations."
    },
    {
      "name": "consumer_segment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.72,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Segment gives a useful axis to see which audiences drive low scores. pet_owner for dog food is well grounded. Default 'general_consumer' for reviews without clear segment cues is acceptable and not fabricated, though it carries little signal in those rows."
    },
    {
      "name": "repeat_purchase_intent",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.72,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Repurchase intent is a strong outcome-oriented axis for prioritizing actions. Grounded where signaled (finicky-dog approval -> high; medicinal disappointment -> low). Some rows lack explicit intent statements yet get confident 'high_repeat_likelihood', a mild over-inference but generally consistent with review sentiment."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.87,
  "source_groundedness": 0.77,
  "rationale": "The added columns collectively give the analyst several genuine, distinct, actionable axes (taste, intensity, value, expectation gaps, segment, repurchase) directly relevant to identifying levers that improve product scores; none are trivial constants or mere restatements. Grounding is mostly sound, with correct use of Unknown/NULL on silent rows for several columns. The main weakness is product_freshness, which asserts positive freshness on rows that never mention it instead of marking unknown, plus milder unsupported positive defaults in price_value_fit and repeat_purchase_intent. Overall solid quality with one notably weakly-grounded column."
}

